    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d931">

                <pb facs="0726" n="610" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg610"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/13510.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Brazilian Minister to Mexico to the Secretary of
                        State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brazilian Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-14T22:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 14, 1914, 10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>187. Department’s No. 281, October 7, 5 p.m. Foreign Office has just advised me
                    that my note, in which I transcribed Department’s said telegram, has, by order
                    of General Carranza, “been transcribed to the Convention of Generals and
                    Governors at Aguascalientes so that said Convention may decide on the same,
                    after which Carranza will give an answer.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cardoso de Oliveira</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
